Pre-copy destination state
--------------------------
The first-hit snapshot is at CPL0 RIP `0xffffffff81999bd4`, immediately before
the CALL into `_copy_to_user`. Both runs pass the artifact-specific gate with
`RDX=7`, `RSI=0xffffffff82fdeb8f`, and `RDI=R13-7`. The `stack_input` and
`copy_source` probes translate in both runs. The `bprm_p` value translates to
the same physical address `0x39fffd2` in each run, with the same present raw PT
leaf `0x39ff867`.

| Run | `bprm_p` | aligned stack pointer (`R13`) | `bprm_p - R13` | copy destination (`RDI`) | destination page | destination walk |
|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|
| 0001 | `0x7fffe29fdfd2` | `0x7fffe29fda80` | `0x552` (1,362 bytes) | `0x7fffe29fda79` | `0x7fffe29fd000` | translated; PT leaf `0x39ff867` |
| 0002 | `0x7fff9d9d8fd2` | `0x7fff9d9d7430` | `0x1ba2` (7,074 bytes) | `0x7fff9d9d7429` | `0x7fff9d9d7000` | `page_fault`; PT index 471 leaf `0x0` |

In run 0001 the copy destination and `bprm_p` are on the same mapped 4 KiB
page. In run 0002 `bprm_p` is on the mapped page beginning at
`0x7fff9d9d8000` (PT index 472), while the destination lies on its adjacent
lower page, whose PT leaf is absent (index 471). The effective pointer
decrements are observed differences after stack alignment; they are not direct
measurements of the RNG output.

Delivered fault correspondence and conclusion
---------------------------------------------
Run 0002's complete event list has one additional event at zero-based index
30: CPL0 write `#PF` at RIP `0xffffffff8271a9c1`, CR2 `0x7fff9d9d7429`, error
code `0x2`. Its recorded original-access walk has present PML4/PDPT/PD entries
and raw-zero PT leaf at index 471. This is the same destination address and
missing leaf already observed by the pre-CALL snapshot. Run 0001 has no event
at its destination address; its pre-CALL destination translation succeeds.

The paired evidence supports the local explanation for this pair's +1
delivered `#PF`: stack alignment placed the seven-byte platform-string copy on
an already mapped argument page in run 0001 and on an absent adjacent lower
page in run 0002; the latter store produced the extra fault. Astra reviewed the
schema-v14 measurements and agrees that no further replay is needed for this
bounded localization.

This does not identify why the stack-alignment result differed, establish the
RNG or prior page-provisioning history, or classify arbitrary page-fault
variation as benign. It does not resolve the source series' strict
`matches=false` result, explain its other machine/UART differences, or satisfy
S5-07 acceptance. No comparator or normalization rule changed. The schema-v14
capture implementation was independently reviewed and committed as `003baf1`.

Counter coverage audit (2026-11-17)
----------------------------------
The exact-pair schema-v14 sidecars were checked for invocation-local counter
coverage. `elf_stack_copy` contains one pre-CALL snapshot (including the input
CR3 and three read-only page walks); it does not contain a post-copy snapshot
or local `cr3_changes`, RDTSC, retired-instruction, or exception deltas. The
sidecar separately contains whole-run page-fault events and aggregate RDTSC
site histograms, while the runner stop contains whole-run endpoint counters.
Those aggregates do not attribute counter changes to this single copy
invocation. This confirms the local extra-`#PF` mechanism but leaves its
invocation-local CR3-counter contribution unmeasured. No new replay was run
for this audit.
